! Half-wave rectifier: a stiff, solution-dependent lumped circuit element
! (a smoothed DIODE) in a transient circuit-coupled magnetodynamics run.
!
! WHY THIS TEST EXISTS
! --------------------
! circuits2D_transient_nonlinear_resistor{,_picard} validate the per-iteration
! export refresh (+ Circuit Variable Relaxation Factor) on a MILD nonlinearity,
! R(I) = 1 + 0.05*I^2. A diode swings its resistance over six decades within a
! fraction of a volt, so it is the stress case that decides whether the Picard
! machinery is enough for rectifier-type TRAFOLO models or whether a
! Newton-based diode component is needed. This test asserts the forward
! conduction peak; the sibling circuits2D_transient_diode_blocking asserts the
! reverse leakage of the very same circuit.
!
! CIRCUIT
!   ideal sinusoidal source -- stranded FEM coil (component 1) -- diode (2)
! all in series. The coil is given an explicit "Resistance = Real 1.0", which
! makes Add_stranded skip the sigma-based FEM resistance, so the coil
! contributes exactly 1 ohm plus its (linear, constant) inductance.
!
! DIODE MODEL (smoothed, VOLTAGE controlled)
!   R(V) = Ron + 0.5*(Roff - Ron)*(1 - tanh(V/Vsm))
! driven by the component's own voltage "crt v 2". Voltage, not current, is the
! control variable: in the blocking state the current is ~1e-4 A and carries
! almost no information about the operating point, whereas the voltage swings
! the full source range.
! Sign convention (verified against a linear run of this same circuit, see
! "CALIBRATION" below): a positive source voltage drives a positive loop
! current and yields a POSITIVE component voltage, so forward bias is
! crt v 2 > 0 and the formula above needs no sign flip.
! tanh is used rather than the algebraic sigmoid V/sqrt(V^2+Vsm^2): the
! algebraic form has a 1/V^2 tail, so R - Ron = Roff*Vsm^2/(4V^2) would still
! be ~390 ohm at the ~0.7 V forward drop reached here, i.e. it would not model
! a diode at all at these parameters. The tanh tail decays as Roff*exp(-2V/Vsm)
! and gives R = 0.178 ohm at the peak. C's tanh saturates instead of
! overflowing, so there is no exp() range problem.
! Ron = 0.1, Roff = 1e5 (kept <= ~1e6 to protect the conditioning of the linear
! system and of the Picard map; the resulting leakage V/(Roff+Rcoil) ~ 1e-4 A
! is asserted by the blocking test, not hidden), Vsm = 0.1 V = 1% of V0.
!
! PICARD TUNING. Sweep run on the FIRST 70 timesteps of this case (t = 0..3.5 s,
! which contains turn-on, the whole conduction lobe and turn-off), everything
! else exactly as below. Reported: number of timesteps that exhaust
! "Steady State Max Iterations = 100", then median / max iterations per step.
!   w = 1.00   -> 70/70 non-converged   (undamped Picard diverges everywhere)
!   w = 0.50   ->  6/70 non-converged
!   w = 0.30   ->  5/70 non-converged
!   w = 0.25   ->  5/70 non-converged
!   w = 0.20   ->  4/70 non-converged
!   w = 0.15   ->  0 non-converged, median 13 / max 33
!   w = 0.125  ->  0 non-converged, median  8 / max 23
!   w = 0.10   ->  0 non-converged, median  7 / max 30   <== used here
!   w = 0.05   ->  0 non-converged, median 17 / max 61   (over-damped)
! Sensitivity to the smoothing width, same 70 timesteps: Vsm = 0.05 with w = 0.10
! still converges everywhere but is slower (median 13.5 / max 30); Vsm = 0.20
! with w = 0.10 converges (median 7 / max 64) but with w = 0.20 or 0.30 it does
! not (5 and 6 non-converged steps) -- i.e. a wider, mushier diode does NOT buy
! a larger relaxation factor here, so there is no reason to widen Vsm.
! This matches the analytic stability bound: the exported-voltage fixed-point
! map V <- R(V)*S/(D + R(V)) has |g'| ~ 6..9 during conduction (S, D are the
! BDF1 source/impedance aggregates), so a contraction needs w < 2/(1+|g'|) ~ 0.2
! and is fastest at w ~ 1/(1+|g'|) ~ 0.11.
!
! LINEAR SYSTEM: a DIRECT solve is used deliberately. With the iterative
! solver the field norm in the blocking phase is ~2.5e-7 while the iterative
! solve's own noise is ~1e-7 RELATIVE, so a relative steady-state criterion
! sits below the noise floor and the coupled loop never converges there (8 of
! 175 timesteps hit the iteration limit, and the resulting corruption put the
! forward peak 35% off). The same UMFPACK direct solve is used by
! circuits2D_transient_foil under the identical HAVE_UMFPACK/HAVE_MUMPS guard.
!
! STEADY STATE CONVERGENCE IS ABSOLUTE: over one period the A-field norm spans
! ~9.9e-3 (conduction) down to ~0 at t = 5.000 s, where the source and the
! blocked current both cross zero exactly on a grid point. A relative criterion
! is 0/0 there and cannot be met at any iteration count. An absolute tolerance
! of 1e-10 corresponds to ~1e-8 relative at the conduction peak, which is far
! tighter than what the assertion below needs.
!
! CALIBRATION (linear cross-check, run before baking the reference norms):
! the identical case with a fixed "Resistance = Real 1.0" on component 2 was
! run for 250 steps. The lumped BDF1 recurrence L*(I_n - I_{n-1})/dt =
! V(t_n) - R*I_n reproduces it with a CONSTANT L: solving for L step by step
! gives min 2.395726 H, max 2.396014 H, median 2.3957830 H, and replaying the
! recurrence with that L matches Elmer to 1.3e-7 A over all 250 steps
! (amplitude ~2.73 A). The continuous-time amplitude V0/sqrt(R^2+(wL)^2) =
! 2.7667 A differs by 1.44%, which is exactly the BDF1 amplitude damping at
! 100 steps/period. Frequency f = 0.2 Hz was then chosen so that
! wL = 3.011 ohm = 2.74 x the conducting loop resistance (1 + Ron), i.e. a
! non-trivial conduction angle (the diode stays on ~1.4 s past the source zero
! crossing).
!
! REFERENCE VALUE
! The asserted number comes from an independent lumped solver (companion file
! diode_reference.py in this directory) that has no code in common with Elmer:
! per step it solves the implicit BDF1 relation for V_D by scanning the residual
! g(V) = R(V)*I(V) - V for sign changes and bisecting. The scan also confirms
! that this circuit has EXACTLY ONE fixed point at every one of the 175 steps,
! so Picard has a unique target and any chatter would be a numerical artifact.
! The reference uses the same BDF1 time grid as Elmer on purpose, so that the
! assertion tests the nonlinear circuit coupling and not the BDF1
! time-discretisation error (a dt refinement in that script puts the continuous
! limit of the forward peak 0.7% above the value asserted here; the reverse
! leakage is insensitive to dt).
! Forward peak of the SECOND period = discrete argmax of the current over
! t in (T, 2T]; it lands on step 142, t = 7.10 s, where the run stops:
!   I_diode = 3.9463754144 A,  V_diode = +0.703095 V,  R_diode = 0.178162 ohm
! Elmer reaches 3.9463753104 A (relative deviation 2.6e-8, as reported by
! CompareToReferenceSolution). Comparing EVERY timestep of the longer sibling
! run (175 steps, identical settings) against the reference, the largest
! deviation anywhere on the trajectory is 1.05e-7 A = 2.7e-8 of the peak -- so
! the agreement is not a coincidence at the asserted instant.
!
! Based on circuits2D_transient_variable_resistor by Eelis Takala, Trafotek Oy.
Check Keywords "Warn"
INCLUDE sif/diode_rectifier_circuit.definitions
INCLUDE 1381/mesh.names
Header 1
   Mesh DB "." "1381"
End

$ V0 = 10        ! source amplitude [V]
$ f  = 0.2       ! source frequency [Hz], chosen so that wL = 2.74*(Rcoil+Ron)
$ T  = 1/f
$ nper = 100     ! timesteps per period
$ dt = T/nper
$ nsteps = 142   ! last step lands exactly on the forward peak of period 2
$ Ron = 0.1      ! diode on-state resistance [ohm]
$ Roff = 1.0e5   ! diode off-state resistance [ohm]
$ Vsm = 0.1      ! diode smoothing voltage [V]
$ wrel = 0.10    ! Circuit Variable Relaxation Factor, see PICARD TUNING above
$ sstol = 1.0e-10

Simulation 1
   Max Output Level = 5
   Coordinate System = Axi symmetric
   Simulation Type = Transient
   Timestepping Method = BDF
   BDF Order = 1
   Timestep Sizes(1) = $ dt
   Timestep Intervals(1) = $ nsteps
!  > 1 enables the within-timestep Picard loop over the nonlinear resistance.
!  Measured on this run: min 4, median 7, mean 6.8, max 30 iterations/timestep
!  and ZERO timesteps that exhaust the limit.
   Steady State Max Iterations = 100
!  Guards against a premature exit while the field is nearly zero (blocking
!  phase and the two zero crossings), where a single coupled iteration can look
!  converged before the nonlinearity has been iterated at all.
   Steady State Min Iterations = 3
End
Solver 1
   Exec Solver = Always
   Equation = Circuits
   Procedure = "CircuitsAndDynamics" "CircuitsAndDynamics"
!  Publish the circuit currents/voltages as mesh variables ("crt i", "crt v")
!  so that component keywords may depend on them.
   Export Circuit Variables = Logical True
   Circuit Variable Relaxation Factor = Real $ wrel
End
Solver 2
   Equation = "Mag"
   Variable = A
   Procedure = "MagnetoDynamics2D" "MagnetoDynamics2D"
   NonLinear System Relaxation Factor=1
   Export Lagrange Multiplier = Logical True
   Linear System Solver = Direct
   Linear System Direct Method = UMFPACK
   Steady State Convergence Tolerance = $ sstol
   Steady State Convergence Absolute = Logical True
End
Solver 3 ! Result output
   Exec Solver = Never
   Equation = "ResultOutput"
   Procedure = "ResultOutputSolve" "ResultOutputSolver"
   Output File Name = 1381-results
   Vtu format = Logical True
   Save Geometry Ids = Logical True
End
Solver 4
   Exec Solver = Always
   Equation = Circuits Output
   Procedure = "CircuitsAndDynamics" "CircuitsOutput"
End
Solver 5
   Exec Solver = After Timestep
   Equation = "sv"
   Procedure = "SaveData" "SaveScalars"
   Filename = 1381/dat/diode_rectifier.dat
!  Saved for diagnostics only. NOTE: with relaxation w < 1 the EXPORTED mesh
!  copy of a circuit variable that does not control any keyword is a first-order
!  filter of the true iterate with pole (1-w), so it converges at rate (1-w) per
!  coupled iteration and still lags by O(0.1%) when the loop exits. Here the
!  diode voltage "crt v 2" is the controlling variable and is converged (it is
!  what the relaxation is tuned for), while the exported "crt i 2" lags -- 3.9445
!  instead of 3.9464 A at the final step. This is a reporting artifact of the
!  export, not a solution error: the solved circuit dofs published by
!  CircuitsOutput ("res: i_component(2)") are exact, which is why the norm below
!  is taken from those and not from "crt i 2".
   Variable 1 = String "crt i 2"
   Variable 2 = String "crt v 2"
   Show Norm Name = String "res: i_component(2)"
End
Equation 1
   Active Solvers(2) = 2 3
End
Equation 2
   Active Solvers(3) = 1 2 3
End
Material 1
   Name = iron
   Electric Conductivity = 0
   Relative Permeability = 2000
End
Material 2
   Name = air
   Electric Conductivity = 0
   Relative Permeability = 1
End
Material 3
   Name = aluminium
   Relative Permeability = 1
   Electric Conductivity = 1e7
End
Body 1
   Name = Core
   Target Bodies(6) = 1 2 3 4 5 6
   Equation = 1
   Material = 1
End
Body 2
   Name = air
   Target Bodies(2) = 9 7
   Equation = 1
   Material = 2
End
Body 3
   Name = test 1
   Target Bodies = 8
   Equation = 2
   Material = 3
End
Component 1
   Name = String test inductor
   Master Bodies = Integer 3
   Coil Type = String stranded
   Number of Turns = Real 1000
!  Explicit coil resistance: suppresses the FEM sigma-based resistance so that
!  the loop resistance is exactly 1 ohm + R_diode.
   Resistance = Real 1.0
End
Component 2
   Name = String diode
   Component Type = String Resistor
!  Smoothed diode: resistance depends on this component's OWN voltage, the
!  second exported component voltage ("crt v 2"). Forward bias (crt v 2 > 0)
!  gives R -> Ron, reverse bias gives R -> Roff.
   Resistance = Variable "crt v 2"
     Real MATC "Ron + 0.5*(Roff-Ron)*(1 - tanh(tx/Vsm))"
End
Body Force 1
   Name = "Circuit"
   testsource = Variable time
     Real MATC "V0*sin(2*pi*f*tx)"
End
Boundary Condition 1
   Target Boundaries(4) = $ center0 center1 center2 center3
   A = Real 0
End
Boundary Condition 2
   Target Boundaries(2) = 4 6
   Name = "Air Gap"
   Air Gap = logical True
   Air Gap Length = Real 0.0001
   Air Gap Relative Permeability = Real 1
End
Boundary Condition 3
   Target Boundaries(7) = 10 13 14 17 18 19 27
   Infinity BC = Logical True
End

Solver 2 :: Reference Norm = 9.86655070E-03
! Independent lumped BDF1 reference at t = 7.10 s, see REFERENCE VALUE above.
! 2e-2 leaves room for round-off differences in the tail of tanh across
! platforms; the deviation actually observed is 2.6e-8.
Solver 5 :: Reference Norm = 3.9463754144
Solver 5 :: Reference Norm Tolerance = Real 2E-02
